# Network Troubleshooting — Common IPv4 Configuration Problems

## Objective

Understand how common IPv4 configuration problems affect network connectivity and how I would identify and troubleshoot them using Windows networking tools.

The main problems covered are:

- No valid IP address
- IP address conflict
- Missing or incorrect default gateway
- Incorrect subnet mask

---

## 1. No Valid IP Address Assigned

If a device does not have a valid IPv4 address, it will not be able to communicate normally with other devices on the network.

One of the first commands I would use is:

ipconfig /all

This allows me to check:

- IPv4 address
- Subnet mask
- Default gateway
- DHCP status
- DHCP server
- DNS server

### APIPA Address

If Windows is configured to use DHCP but cannot obtain normal IPv4 configuration, it may assign itself an APIPA address.

APIPA addresses use the range:

169.254.x.x

If I see a `169.254.x.x` address, I would suspect an address-assignment/DHCP problem.

### Possible Causes

- DHCP server unavailable
- DHCP server cannot be reached
- Ethernet cable disconnected
- Wi-Fi disconnected
- Network adapter disabled
- Incorrect VLAN or network connection
- DHCP client/service problem

### Troubleshooting

First I would check the physical or wireless connection and confirm that the network adapter is enabled.

I would then run:

ipconfig /all

If DHCP should be used, I would confirm:

DHCP Enabled: Yes

I could then attempt to obtain a new DHCP lease:

ipconfig /release

ipconfig /renew

If the device still receives an APIPA address, I would continue investigating why it cannot reach or receive configuration from DHCP.

### Key Troubleshooting Clue

169.254.x.x → Think DHCP/address-assignment problem.

